#: ../../../tasks/task_types/cmd.rst:2
msgid "``cmd`` tasks"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/task_types/cmd.rst:4
msgid ""
"**Command tasks** contain a single command that will be executed as a sub-"
"process without a shell. This covers most basic use cases such as the "
"following examples."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/task_types/cmd.rst:6
msgid ""
"[tool.poe.tasks.test]\n"
"cmd = \"pytest -v tests\""
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/task_types/cmd.rst:13
msgid ""
"Tasks defined as just a string value, are interpreted as ``cmd`` tasks by "
"default."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/task_types/cmd.rst:17
msgid "Available task options"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/task_types/cmd.rst:19
msgid ""
"``cmd`` tasks support all of the :doc:`standard task options <../options>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/task_types/cmd.rst:21
msgid "The following options are also accepted:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/task_types/cmd.rst:23
msgid ""
"**ignore_fail** : ``bool`` | ``list[int]``  :ref:`📖<Ignore task failure>`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/task_types/cmd.rst:22
msgid "bool | list[int]  📖"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/task_types/cmd.rst:24
msgid ""
"Return exit code 0 even if the task fails, or specify a list of task exit "
"codes to ignore."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/task_types/cmd.rst:26
msgid ""
"**empty_glob** : ``Literal[\"pass\", \"null\", \"fail\"]`` :ref:`📖<Glob "
"expansion>`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/task_types/cmd.rst:25
msgid "Literal[\"pass\", \"null\", \"fail\"] 📖"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/task_types/cmd.rst:27
msgid ""
"Determines how to handle glob patterns with no matches. The default is "
"``\"pass\"``, which causes unmatched patterns to be passed through to the "
"command (just like in bash). Setting it to ``\"null\"`` will replace an "
"unmatched pattern with nothing, and setting it to ``\"fail\"`` will cause "
"the task to fail with an error if there are no matches."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/task_types/cmd.rst:31
msgid "Shell like features"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/task_types/cmd.rst:33
msgid ""
"It is important to understand that ``cmd`` tasks are executed without a "
"shell (to maximize portability). However some shell like features are still "
"available including basic parameter expansion and pattern matching. Quotes "
"and escapes are also generally interpreted as one would expect in a shell."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/task_types/cmd.rst:38
msgid "Referencing environment variables"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/task_types/cmd.rst:40
msgid ""
"Environment variables can be templated into the command. Just like in bash: "
"whitespace inside a variable results in a word break, and glob patterns are "
"evaluated after parameter expansion, unless the parameter expansion is "
"inside double quotes. Single quotes disable parameter expansion. Curly "
"braces are recommended but optional."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/task_types/cmd.rst:42
msgid ""
"[tool.poe.tasks]\n"
"greet = \"echo Hello ${USER}\""
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/task_types/cmd.rst:47
msgid ""
"$ poe greet\n"
"Poe => echo Hello nat\n"
"Hello nat"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/task_types/cmd.rst:53
msgid ""
"Parameter expansion can also be disabled by escaping the $ with a backslash "
"like so:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/task_types/cmd.rst:55
msgid ""
"[tool.poe.tasks]\n"
"greet = \"echo Hello \\\\$USER\"  # the backslash itself needs escaping for "
"the toml parser"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/task_types/cmd.rst:63
msgid "Parameter expansion operators"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/task_types/cmd.rst:65
msgid ""
"When referencing an environment variable in a cmd task you can use the ``:-"
"`` operator from bash to specify a *default value*, to be used in case the "
"variable is unset. Similarly the ``:+`` operator can be used to specify an "
"*alternate value* to use in place of the environment variable if it *is* set."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/task_types/cmd.rst:67
msgid ""
"In the following example, if ``AWS_REGION`` has a value then it will be "
"used, otherwise ``us-east-1`` will be used as a fallback."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/task_types/cmd.rst:69
msgid ""
"[tool.poe.tasks]\n"
"tables = \"aws dynamodb list-tables --region ${AWS_REGION:-us-east-1}\""
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/task_types/cmd.rst:74
msgid ""
"The ``:+`` or *alternate value* operator is especially useful in cases such "
"as the following where you might want to control whether some CLI options "
"are passed to the command."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/task_types/cmd.rst:76
msgid ""
"In this example we declare a boolean argument with no default, so if the ``--"
"arn-only`` flag is provided to the task then three additional CLI options "
"will be included in the task content. If not then _arn_only will evaluate to "
"empty string in the context of the cmd task content."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/task_types/cmd.rst:78
msgid ""
"[tool.poe.tasks.aws-identity]\n"
"cmd = \"aws sts get-caller-identity ${_arn_only:+ --no-cli-pager --output "
"text --query 'Arn'}\"\n"
"args = [{ name = \"_arn_only\", options = [\"--arn-only\"], type = "
"\"boolean\" }]"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/task_types/cmd.rst:84
msgid ""
"When you want to switch the value based on whether a flag is present, it’s "
"best to use the ``:-`` operator."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/task_types/cmd.rst:86
msgid ""
"In the example below, it prints ``\"hello!\"`` if the ``--hello`` flag is "
"present; otherwise, it prints ``\"hi!\"``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/task_types/cmd.rst:88
msgid ""
"[tool.poe.tasks.greet]\n"
"cmd = \"echo ${hello:-hello!}\"\n"
"args = [{ name = \"hello\", type = \"boolean\", default = \"hi!\" }]"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/task_types/cmd.rst:96
msgid ""
"Unlike bash, glob patterns (``*``, ``?``, ``[...]``) inside ``:-`` and ``:"
"+`` operator arguments are treated as literal text and are not expanded."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/task_types/cmd.rst:99
msgid "Glob expansion"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/task_types/cmd.rst:101
msgid ""
"Glob patterns in cmd tasks are expanded and replaced with the list of "
"matching files and directories. Glob patterns are evaluated relative to the "
"working directory of the task."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/task_types/cmd.rst:103
msgid ""
"The supported glob syntax is that of the |glob_link|, which differs from "
"bash in that square bracket patterns don't support character classes, don't "
"break on whitespace, and don't allow escaping of contained characters."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/task_types/cmd.rst:132
msgid ""
"<a href=\"https://docs.python.org/3/library/glob.html\" "
"target=\"_blank\">python standard library glob module</a>"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/task_types/cmd.rst:105
msgid ""
"If there are no matches then by default the pattern is passed through to the "
"command unchanged (just like in bash). This behavior can be overridden for a "
"specific task by setting the :toml:`empty_glob` option to ``\"null\"`` or "
"``\"fail\"``. If set to ``\"null\"`` then the pattern will be replaced with "
"nothing (similar to how bash behaves when |nullglob_link| is set), and if "
"set to ``\"fail\"`` then a glob pattern with no matches will cause task "
"execution to fail with an error."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/task_types/cmd.rst:136
msgid ""
"<a href=\"https://www.gnu.org/software/bash/manual/html_node/Filename-"
"Expansion.html\" target=\"_blank\">nullglob option</a>"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/task_types/cmd.rst:107
msgid ""
"The following task uses glob patterns to specify all ``.pyc`` files and "
"``__pycache__`` directories in the project for removal, and thanks to the :"
"toml:`empty_glob` option it will succeed even if there are no matches since "
"no arguments will be passed to the ``rm`` command."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/task_types/cmd.rst:109
msgid ""
"[tool.poe.tasks.clean]\n"
"cmd = \"\"\"\n"
"rm -rf ./**/*.pyc\n"
"       ./**/__pycache__    # this will match all __pycache__ dirs in the "
"project\n"
"\"\"\"\n"
"empty_glob = \"null\""
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/task_types/cmd.rst:118
msgid ""
"$ poe clean\n"
"Poe => rm -rf ./tests/__pycache__ ./docs/__pycache__ ..."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/task_types/cmd.rst:125
msgid ""
"Notice that this example also demonstrates that comments and excess "
"whitespace (including new lines) are ignored, without needing to escape new "
"lines."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/task_types/cmd.rst:129
msgid ""
"Just like in bash, the glob pattern can be escaped by wrapping it in quotes, "
"or preceding it with a backslash."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/task_types/cmd.rst:142
msgid "Ignore task failure"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/task_types/cmd.rst:146
msgid ""
"This option works the same for all *Execution task types* including :doc:"
"`cmd<cmd>`, :doc:`script<script>`, :doc:`expr<expr>`, and :doc:"
"`shell<shell>`, but has a slightly different interpretation for :doc:"
"`sequence<sequence>`, :doc:`parallel<parallel>`, and :doc:`ref<ref>` tasks."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/task_types/cmd.rst:148
msgid ""
"Normally if a task subprocess returns a non-zero exit code, then the task is "
"considered to have failed. This failure propagates to the parent task (if "
"any), and ultimately poe will return the same exit code to the host shell. "
"However it is possible to configure a task to ignore failure, and return "
"zero regardless, by setting the ``ignore_fail`` option like so:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/task_types/cmd.rst:150
msgid ""
"[tool.poe.tasks.clean]\n"
"cmd         = \"rm -rf ./src/**/*.pyc\"\n"
"ignore_fail = true"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/task_types/cmd.rst:156
msgid ""
"You can also ignore task failures just in case of one or more specific exit "
"codes by providing a list of integers:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/task_types/cmd.rst:158
msgid ""
"[tool.poe.tasks.serve]\n"
"cmd        = \"pytest\"\n"
"ignore_fail = [4, 5] # don't fail if no tests are found"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/task_types/cmd.rst:166
msgid "Controlling placement of free arguments via ``$POE_EXTRA_ARGS``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/task_types/cmd.rst:168
msgid ""
"By default, any free arguments passed to a ``cmd`` task are appended to the "
"end of the command. However, if you reference ``$POE_EXTRA_ARGS`` explicitly "
"in the command string, poe will expand it in place instead, and *not* "
"additionally append the extra args at the end. This is useful when fixed "
"options must follow the free arguments."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/task_types/cmd.rst:170
msgid ""
"See the :ref:`forwarding-free-arguments-via-poe-extra-args` section of the "
"args guide for details and examples."
msgstr ""
